  "answer": "I'll look through Shopizer's storefront cart API and how store merchant configuration is loaded so the quantity cap can be enforced per store.The cap belongs on the cart writes themselves, read from merchant configuration on each request so a store change applies immediately. I'll reject any line that would end up above that limit before the cart is saved.The storefront cart API now rejects any create, add, or quantity change that would leave a line with more than N units. N comes from the store's `CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY` merchant configuration (default 99) and is read on each request, so a change applies without a restart. A quantity of 0 still removes the line. Over-limit calls return HTTP 400 and the cart is not saved.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-shop -am test -Dtest=CartLineQuantityLimitTest -DfailIfNoTests=false -q\"], \"result\": \"passed\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"HTTP 400 responses from POST /api/v1/cart, PUT /api/v1/cart/{code}, and POST /api/v1/cart/{code}/multi against a running store, including that the persisted cart is unchanged and that updating CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY applies without a restart\"], \"missing\": [\"A documented way to run the shopping-cart API integration tests with a store and a CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY merchant configuration\"]}",
